blication","claim_ceiling_restated":"Runs examples of receipt validation, replay scope, proof contracts, process classification, state convergence, trace recording, dependency impact and doctrine drift, and checks each copied source against its manifest digest, rejecting a receipt with provider-context drift and a proof contract containing `sorry`.","claim_id":"claim::batch5_authority_systems_capsule","evidence_class":"verified_macro_body_import","evidence_strength_rank":5,"organ_id":"batch5_authority_systems_capsule","truth_accounting_bucket":"copied_non_secret_macro_body","validator_command":"python -m microcosm_core.organs.batch5_authority_systems_capsule run --input fixtures/first_wave/batch5_authority_systems_capsule/input --out receipts/first_wave/batch5_authority_systems_capsule --acceptance-out receipts/acceptance/first_wave/batch5_authority_systems_capsule_fixture_acceptance.json"},"claim_clause_coverage":{"claim_text_sha256":"ec45a494b004ce558ae3c11a0128a09cff12053a225434c3a3a490a1827860f3","clauses":[],"mapped_clause_count":null,"status":"unknown","whole_claim_coverage":"unknown"},"classification":{"evidence_class":"verified_macro_body_import","family":"import_projection_and_drift","real_substrate_disposition":"real_substrate_capsule","truth_accounting_bucket":"copied_non_secret_macro_body"},"component_id":"batch5_authority_systems_capsule","evidence_state":{"current_backend_binding":{"status":"not_checked"},"fresh_prover":{"status":"not_recorded_by_index"},"fresh_replay":{"status":"not_recorded_by_index"},"index_state":"retained_generated_snapshot"},"guide":{"availability":"available","guide_id":"paper_module.batch5_authority_systems_capsule","plain":"Systems that keep saved records and run programs need safeguards that catch mistakes. It is given made-up cases for saved records and background processes, plus a map of what depends on what. It runs each safeguard on its cases and rejects the bad ones, and for the dependency map it lists everything that relies on a chosen item, directly or indirectly. The output is an outcome for each case, so someone can check each safeguard did what it should. One small step, finding what relies on the chosen item, is checked by a mathematical proof against written rules.","plain_availability":"available","ref":"paper_modules/batch5_authority_systems_capsule.md","sha256":"6f0fb9297fbb514be9b1bb4a6e1fbac930e6d1fee9bc9d5cc0e62183fe51920e","text":"# Exercise receipt and execution safeguards\n\n> **In plain terms.** Systems that keep saved records and run programs need safeguards that catch mistakes. It is given made-up cases for saved records and background processes, plus a map of what depends on what. It runs each safeguard on its cases and rejects the bad ones, and for the dependency map it lists everything that relies on a chosen item, directly or indirectly. The output is an outcome for each case, so someone can check each safeguard did what it should. One small step, finding what relies on the chosen item, is checked by a mathematical proof against written rules.\n\nThis fixture runs examples of receipt validation, replay scope, proof-contract checks, process classification, state convergence, trace recording, dependency impact and doctrine drift. It records each outcome alongside source-manifest evidence.\n\n## Run the example\n\nFrom the repository root, with Python 3.11 or newer and the project dependencies installed:\n\n```sh\nPYTHONPATH=src python3 -m microcosm_core.organs.batch5_authority_systems_capsule run \\\n  --input fixtures/first_wave/batch5_authority_systems_capsule/input \\\n  --out .microcosm/component-guides/batch5_authority_systems_capsule/result \\\n  --acceptance-out .microcosm/component-guides/batch5_authority_systems_capsule/acceptance.json --card\n```\n\nThe command prints a compact JSON card and writes its results under `.microcosm/component-guides/`. A passing run returns exit code 0; a blocked run returns 1.\n\n## What this checks\n\nA receipt with provider-context drift is rejected, and a proof contract containing `sorry` is rejected. A live descendant must not be signalled by the orphan classifier. When a generated projection remains unresolved and its source signature changes between checks, the fixture classifies it as a source-moved residual.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe dependency-impact exercise calls a [Dafny-verified reachability core](../formal/components/dependency_reachability/README.md). For the supplied finite graph it returns exactly the nodes connected to the starting dependency by one or more dependency edges, including the start only if a cycle reaches it. Python constructs the graph; the proof does not establish that its edges are complete or verify the other exercises.\n\nThe direct fixture runs this core. `make component-replays` separately applies the [shared admission check](../formal/replay/README.md) to the component replay’s captured observations.\n\n## Limits\n\nRuns examples of receipt validation, replay scope, proof contracts, process classification, state convergence, trace recording, dependency impact and doctrine drift, and checks each copied source against its manifest digest, rejecting a receipt with provider-context drift and a proof contract containing `sorry`.\n\n## Source and evidence\n\nRead the [implementation](../src/microcosm_core/organs/batch5_authority_systems_capsule.py), [fixture input](../fixtures/first_wave/batch5_authority_systems_capsule/input/batch5_exercise_manifest.json), and [focused tests](../tests/test_batch5_authority_systems_capsule.py). The [saved acceptance result](../receipts/acceptance/first_wave/batch5_authority_systems_capsule_fixture_acceptance.json) is historical sample evidence, not the result of your run. The [JSON record](batch5_authority_systems_capsule.json) identifies the module; the canonical capsule metadata is in [paper_module_capsules.json](../core/paper_module_capsules.json).\n\n[W3C PROV](https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-overview/) and [SLSA provenance](https://slsa.dev/spec/v1.2/provenance) provide related provenance models.\n"},"proofs":[{"claim_ids":["claim::batch5_authority_systems_capsule"],"component_ids":["batch5_authority_systems_capsule"],"current_backend_binding":{"status":"not_checked"},"fresh_prover":{"status":"not_recorded_by_index"},"guide":{"availability":"available","plain":null,"plain_availability":"unrecorded","ref":"formal/components/dependency_reachability/README.md","sha256":"37139689ba63fe5be87c1c2a05304c2d9badf82ebbfc06934af67692ab1bf70e","text":"# Dependency reachability\n\n`DependencyReachability.dfy` specifies paths independently of the computation:\na path is a sequence of at least two vertices whose consecutive pairs are\nedges. `Compute` returns exactly the vertices reached by such paths from the\nstart. It terminates on every finite input graph, including graphs with cycles.\nThe start is returned only when a positive-length cycle reaches it.\n\nThe Batch 5 evaluator converts each dependency relation to an edge from the\ndependency to its dependent. Its adapter maps normalized strings to integers,\ninvokes the generated core and sorts the decoded strings. The theorem concerns\nthat finite graph; it does not establish that the graph contains all real\nrelationships. Python normalization, JSON transport, the isolated worker,\ncompiler and host remain trusted. The worker also has request and time limits.\n\nThe proof derives soundness by extending path witnesses and completeness from\nclosure under edges. Its loop decreases the finite set of vertices not yet\nreached. `negative/OneHopMutant.dfy` replaces transitive expansion with an\nempty set while retaining the theorem; verification rejects the mutant. The\nfocused tests exercise cycles, absent nodes, normalization and the Batch 5 call\npath as well as comparisons with an independent traversal.\n\nThe recorded [metadata](metadata.json) binds the source, mutant and generated\nPython hashes to Dafny 4.11.0. Recheck with the repository's component-kernel\nchecker and the [focused tests](../../../tests/test_component_dependency_reachability.py).\n"},"index_state":"retained_generated_snapshot","modeled_contracts":[{"assumptions":"Trusted host supplies normalized observations; adapter, isolated worker, Dafny, compiler and runtime remain trusted.","contract_sha256":"a1d8cc84b5966fddd20ea66f7b6f5bdfff555593b9eb0e304548a186fba117a2","descriptor_origin":"metadata_contract","entry_theorem_refs":["formal/components/dependency_reachability/DependencyReachability.dfy#Compute","formal/components/dependency_reachability/DependencyReachability.dfy#PathExtension","formal/components/dependency_reachability/DependencyReachability.dfy#ClosedWalk"],"input_model":"Finite directed graph with normalized integer IDs; the caller points edges from dependency to dependent.","limits":"The supplied graph may omit real dependencies; host normalization and graph construction are not proved.","requirements_review":{"status":"unrecorded"},"specification_refs":["formal/components/dependency_reachability/DependencyReachability.dfy#Walk","formal/components/dependency_reachability/DependencyReachability.dfy#Reach"],"statement":"The result is exactly the vertices reachable from the start by a path of at least one edge.","subclaim_id":"subclaim::dependency_reachability"}],"proof":{"adapter_ref":"src/plectis/components/dependency_reachability/_adapter.py","claim_ids":["claim::batch5_authority_systems_capsule"],"component_ids":["batch5_authority_systems_capsule"],"current_binding":{"assessment_time_basis":"index_generation_only","checker_ref":"scripts/check/check_component_kernels.py#check_binding","generated_python":{"DependencyReachability.py":"f7cf51c59e08afcafb9823f9dea328262e85b9203612dfd1fc364c2c61581662","System_/__init__.py":"96b6e33c036bc3aeab6d1810c7aef085184d09c407bdec032ea89d7a98843edf","__main__.py":"8075caf9cb80308e2c46d9383778b910b88342ab1587a6082926f93fe0d9111c","_dafny/__init__.py":"18d39decdedad0e188456e15d17f1dcd5113c4abc258ad6cb718e39534555e7a","module_.py":"4c118c93edadcb4d82e97bf26c14ce9233829fed18f8d6d75d02649e5805589d"},"source_sha256":"8bbf881721beef39fea61585811654eeaa39dff2f36b13ba10deeabb922b0892","status":"current_hash_binding_pass"},"entry_theorem_refs":["formal/components/dependency_reachability/DependencyReachability.dfy#Compute","formal/components/dependency_reachability/DependencyReachability.dfy#PathExtension","formal/components/dependency_reachability/DependencyReachability.dfy#ClosedWalk"],"excerpts":[{"kind":"predicate","line_end":14,"line_start":12,"name":"Walk","ref":"formal/components/dependency_reachability/DependencyReachability.dfy#Walk","source_sha256":"8bbf881721beef39fea61585811654eeaa39dff2f36b13ba10deeabb922b0892","text":"  ghost predicate Walk(es: seq<Edge>, p: seq<int>) {\n    |p| >= 2 && forall i :: 0 <= i < |p|-1 ==> Edge(p[i], p[i+1]) in es\n  }\n","text_sha256":"808a56fcf28fdbd35c300833b67f11a5577aa238b7ec091fed1e7023bbac142c"},{"kind":"predicate","line_end":18,"line_start":16,"name":"Reach","ref":"formal/components/dependency_reachability/DependencyReachability.dfy#Reach","source_sha256":"8bbf881721beef39fea61585811654eeaa39dff2f36b13ba10deeabb922b0892","text":"  ghost predicate Reach(es: seq<Edge>, s: int, v: int) {\n    exists p: seq<int> :: Walk(es,p) && p[0] == s && p[|p|-1] == v\n  }\n","text_sha256":"51d3cb49c8099dc82578f1fe9b955359f584a65910765e3e488dd31f597f374a"},{"kind":"method","line_end":112,"line_start":72,"name":"Compute","ref":"formal/components/dependency_reachability/DependencyReachability.dfy#Compute","source_sha256":"8bbf881721beef39fea61585811654eeaa39dff2f36b13ba10deeabb922b0892","text":"  method Compute(es: seq<Edge>, s: int) returns (reached: set<int>)\n    ensures forall v :: v in reached <==> Reach(es,s,v)\n  {\n    reached := Expand(es,{s});\n    forall v | v in reached\n      ensures Reach(es,s,v)\n    {\n      assert Edge(s,v) in es;\n      assert Walk(es,[s,v]);\n    }\n    var added: set<int> := {};\n    while true\n      invariant reached <= Vertices(es)\n      invariant forall v :: v in reached ==> Reach(es,s,v)\n      invariant forall v :: Edge(s,v) in es ==> v in reached\n      decreases Vertices(es) - reached\n    {\n      added := Expand(es,reached);\n      if added <= reached { break; }\n      forall v | v in added - reached\n        ensures Reach(es,s,v)\n      {\n        var u :| u in reached && Edge(u,v) in es;\n        PathExtension(es,s,u,v);\n      }\n      assert reached < reached + added;\n      assert Vertices(es) - (reached + added) < Vertices(es) - reached;\n      reached := reached + added;\n    }\n    assert forall u,v :: u in reached && Edge(u,v) in es ==> v in reached by {\n      forall u,v | u in reached && Edge(u,v) in es\n        ensures v in reached\n      { assert v in added; }\n    }\n    forall v | Reach(es,s,v)\n      ensures v in reached\n    {\n      var p :| Walk(es,p) && p[0] == s && p[|p|-1] == v;\n      ClosedWalk(es,p,reached);\n    }\n  }\n","text_sha256":"492044124e4cd51edce2e25c248369805c0d57e1dbc7ef4c35459ea8ac4f530b"},{"kind":"lemma","line_end":53,"line_start":46,"name":"PathExtension","ref":"formal/components/dependency_reachability/DependencyReachability.dfy#PathExtension","source_sha256":"8bbf881721beef39fea61585811654eeaa39dff2f36b13ba10deeabb922b0892","text":"  lemma PathExtension(es: seq<Edge>, s: int, u: int, v: int)\n    requires Reach(es,s,u) && Edge(u,v) in es\n    ensures Reach(es,s,v)\n  {\n    var p :| Walk(es,p) && p[0] == s && p[|p|-1] == u;\n    var q := p + [v];\n    assert Walk(es,q);\n  }\n","text_sha256":"16a331618321940a03959343aacc081d4ee69b7fb88c14068cc6fef4baa1d6d5"},{"kind":"lemma","line_end":70,"line_start":55,"name":"ClosedWalk","ref":"formal/components/dependency_reachability/DependencyReachability.dfy#ClosedWalk","source_sha256":"8bbf881721beef39fea61585811654eeaa39dff2f36b13ba10deeabb922b0892","text":"  lemma ClosedWalk(es: seq<Edge>, p: seq<int>, reached: set<int>)\n    requires Walk(es,p)\n    requires forall v :: Edge(p[0],v) in es ==> v in reached\n    requires forall u,v :: u in reached && Edge(u,v) in es ==> v in reached\n    ensures p[|p|-1] in reached\n    decreases |p|\n  {\n    if |p| == 2 {\n      assert Edge(p[0],p[1]) in es;\n    } else {\n      var q := p[..|p|-1];\n      assert Walk(es,q);\n      ClosedWalk(es,q,reached);\n      assert Edge(p[|p|-2],p[|p|-1]) in es;\n    }\n  }\n","text_sha256":"255940dff188fb34d9e4f5b067f35389d49166a64df1ae712fdae56608850888"}],"fresh_prover_result":{"status":"not_run_by_index"},"generated_refs":["src/plectis/components/dependency_reachability/_generated/DependencyReachability.py","src/plectis/components/dependency_reachability/_generated/System_/__init__.py","src/plectis/components/dependency_reachability/_generated/__main__.py","src/plectis/components/dependency_reachability/_generated/_dafny/__init__.py","src/plectis/components/dependency_reachability/_generated/module_.py"],"guide":{"availability":"available","plain":null,"plain_availability":"unrecorded","ref":"formal/components/dependency_reachability/README.md","sha256":"37139689ba63fe5be87c1c2a05304c2d9badf82ebbfc06934af67692ab1bf70e","text":"# Dependency reachability\n\n`DependencyReachability.dfy` specifies paths independently of the computation:\na path is a sequence of at least two vertices whose consecutive pairs are\nedges. `Compute` returns exactly the vertices reached by such paths from the\nstart. It terminates on every finite input graph, including graphs with cycles.\nThe start is returned only when a positive-length cycle reaches it.\n\nThe Batch 5 evaluator converts each dependency relation to an edge from the\ndependency to its dependent. Its adapter maps normalized strings to integers,\ninvokes the generated core and sorts the decoded strings. The theorem concerns\nthat finite graph; it does not establish that the graph contains all real\nrelationships. Python normalization, JSON transport, the isolated worker,\ncompiler and host remain trusted. The worker also has request and time limits.\n\nThe proof derives soundness by extending path witnesses and completeness from\nclosure under edges. Its loop decreases the finite set of vertices not yet\nreached. `negative/OneHopMutant.dfy` replaces transitive expansion with an\nempty set while retaining the theorem; verification rejects the mutant. The\nfocused tests exercise cycles, absent nodes, normalization and the Batch 5 call\npath as well as comparisons with an independent traversal.\n\nThe recorded [metadata](metadata.json) binds the source, mutant and generated\nPython hashes to Dafny 4.11.0. Recheck with the repository's component-kernel\nchecker and the [focused tests](../../../tests/test_component_dependency_reachability.py).\n"},"language":"dafny","metadata_ref":"formal/components/dependency_reachability/metadata.json","module":"DependencyReachability","negative_controls":[{"changed_lines":[{"line":89,"mutated":"      added := {}; // MUTANT: return only direct dependents","original":"      added := Expand(es,reached);"}],"changed_lines_sha256":"0f986cdee766cc39dcb41a05cf00ac07c2332a598d1399d88f2e8b9b8b1205ca","description":"Replaces 'added := Expand(es,reached);' with 'added := {}; // MUTANT: return only direct dependents'.","description_basis":"retained_mutation_recipe","fresh_result":{"status":"not_run_by_index"},"retained_result":{"basis":"recorded_metadata","errors":1,"verified_tasks":9},"source_ref":"formal/components/dependency_reachability/negative/OneHopMutant.dfy","source_sha256":"e3755777e4c3f41dcfabece95b805f107c86b8d2f960ba42a241ab697fb71a45"}],"proof_id":"proof::dafny::dependency_reachability","retained_verification":{"basis":"recorded_metadata","dafny_version":"4.11.0+fcb2042d6d043a2634f0854338c08feeaaaf4ae2","tasks":[],"tasks_availability":"unrecorded","tasks_reason":"Retained metadata records a verified task count, but no task names or kinds.","verified_tasks":10},"trusted_host_refs":["src/plectis/components/_bridge.py","src/ple